In France, the voting process has traditionally been a low-tech experience: voters isolate themselves in a voting booth, mark their preferred candidate on a pre-printed paper sheet, and then place it into an envelope.
After the official verifies the voter's identity, the voter places the envelope into the ballot box and signs the voting roll.
The French electoral law strictly regulates these procedures.
Since 1988, ballot boxes must be transparent, so that voters and observers can see that no envelope was present at the beginning of voting, and no envelope has been added except for those of authorized voters, which have been properly counted.
Candidates have the right to send their representatives to supervise every aspect of this process. In the evening, volunteers conduct the vote counting under strict supervision and specific regulations.
