When a small group of organisms (a small population) becomes separated from a large population (for example, if they cross a mountain range or a river, or if they move to a new island, then they cannot easily return), they will often find themselves in a different environment than the one they previously had.
In a new environment, there are different types of resources and different competitors. Therefore, the population will need different features or variations for strong competition, compared to those they previously needed.
The original population has not changed at all, and they still require the same alignment as before.
As time passes, just as the new settlement adapts to its new environment, it gradually becomes less visible, like the other settlements.
Eventually, after thousands or perhaps millions of years, these two populations will look so different that they can no longer be considered the same species.
We call this process speciation, which means the formation of new species only. Speciation is an inevitable outcome and a very important part of evolution.
