Just as Paris is known today as a fashion center, Constantinople was regarded as the fashion center of feudal Europe.
It is known to have begun around 400 CE as a center of luxury and extravagance, and it remained in existence until the 11th century CE.
In the twelfth century, the main reason for its decline was that the Crusaders returned home carrying silks and spices as gifts, which were worth more than the prices offered in the Byzantine markets.
This was the time when the title of fashion capital shifted from Constantinople to Paris.
The Gothic style flourished between the 10th and 11th centuries and the 14th century.
Clothing was initially greatly influenced by Byzantine culture in the East.
However, due to the slow communication system, the West could lag behind by 25 to 30 years.
At the end of the Middle Ages, Western Europe began to develop its style. One of the most significant developments at that time was the popularization of the practice of using buttons to fasten the clothing of the Crusaders.
Subsistence agriculture is farming carried out solely to provide food up to the extent required to meet the needs of the farmer and his family.
Sustainable agriculture is typically simple and often organic, combining crop rotation or other relatively straightforward practices with the use of locally adapted, protected seeds to achieve maximum yield within an ecological system.
Historically, most farmers have been engaged in agriculture for livelihood, and even today, this remains the case in many developing countries.
Subcultures bring together like-minded individuals who feel overlooked by social standards and provide them with a sense of identity.
The subculture may be specific due to the age, ethnicity, social class, location, and/or gender of its members.
The characteristics that distinguish a subculture may be a combination of linguistic, aesthetic, religious, political, sexual, geographical, or other factors.
Members of a subculture often indicate their membership through a distinctive and symbolic style, body fashion, a special manner, and distinctive speech.
One of the most common methods used to highlight social significance is identifying, during children's upbringing, a few unpleasant events in which children were not allowed to participate in social activities, having been ignored by adults, either by accident or deliberately, thus becoming victims of mistreatment.
Children like this are called "feral" or "wild." Some feral children are confined by people (usually their parents); in some cases, parents distance themselves from the child because they reject the child's severe mental or physical disability.
The wild children must have faced severe mistreatment or trauma before being left behind or running away.
Other individuals are reportedly raised by animals; it has been said about some that they are living in the forest, in their dens.
When raised completely by non-human animals, wild children (in psychological boundaries) nearly completely exhibit behaviors similar to those of the animals that raised them, such as showing fear of humans or indifference toward them.
