In the wide field outside, it is difficult for orangutans, which have a human-like digestive system, to meet their amino acid requirements from the available plant resources.
Moreover, failure to do so may have dangerous consequences: reduced growth, nutritional deficiency, and ultimately death.
The leaves and seeds of plants, which are the most easily accessible resources, contain protein, but they are difficult to digest for creatures like us unless they are cooked.
On the contrary, animal food (ants, termites, eggs) is not only easy to digest, but also contains high levels of protein, as well as all essential amino acids.
After considering all these factors, we should not be surprised if our own ancestors solved their "protein problem" in much the same way that orangutans do today, across vast areas.
Waking up during your normal sleep periods and then falling back asleep (within 10–60 minutes) is a purposeful process.
