In Britain, wagons were built along the roads in the early 16th century.
Although wagon wheels consisted merely of parallel wooden planks, they helped horses pull it quickly and enabled them to carry a slightly heavier load over the poor roads of that era.
Cross-ties were initially used to keep the rails in place. However, it was gradually discovered that the rails would be more effective if iron spikes were placed on top of them.
This method became common, but due to iron, more embellishment began to be made on the wheels of wooden wagons.
Finally, the lattice wheels were replaced with iron wheels. The first complete iron rails were introduced in 1767.
The first form of locomotion was walking. Humans began walking upright simultaneously with the emergence of Homo erectus (meaning "upright man") two million years ago.
Their ancestors, Australopithecus (southern man), typically did not walk upright.
