After the Congress adopted it on July 4, a handwritten draft, signed by John Hancock, the president of the Congress, and Charles Thomson, the secretary, was sent to John Dunlap's printing shop, located a few blocks away.
Throughout the night, copies were prepared between 150 and 200, now known as the "Dunlap Broadside."
The first reading of the document was held on 8 July in the courtyard of Freedom Hall by John Nixan.
A letter was sent to George Washington on July 6, who read it in front of his troops in New York on July 9. A copy of it reached London on August 10.
The 25 Dunlap Broadside copies whose existence is still known are the oldest surviving copies of this document. The original manuscript is lost.
