The Sundarbans is the largest coastal mangrove belt in the world, stretching 80 kilometers (50 miles) from the coastal areas into the remote, underdeveloped regions of Bangladesh and India.
The Sundarbans have been designated as a UNESCO World Heritage site. The forest area located in the Indian region is known as Sundarbans National Park.
Forests are not just swampy mangrove areas — they also include the last remaining strips of great forests that once surrounded the Gangetic plain.
The Sundarbans covers an area of 3,850 km², of which one-third consists of water/mangrove regions.
